 ## Overview
-Neon Raymarcher is a real-time WebGL2 renderer that generates an infinite procedural synthwave city using signed-distance fields (SDFs) within a single fullscreen fragment shader, requiring no meshes, textures, or scene graph [S1, unofficial]. It distinguishes itself from general-purpose SDF renderers by enforcing a strict deterministic hashing contract between CPU and GPU to ensure consistency between the rendered visual scene and the underlying game logic [S1, unofficial].
+Neon Raymarcher is a real-time WebGL2 renderer that generates an infinite procedural synthwave city using signed-distance fields (SDFs) within a single fullscreen fragment shader, requiring no meshes, textures, or scene graph [S1, unofficial]. It distinguishes itself from general-purpose SDF renderers by employing a deterministic integer-hashing contract shared between CPU and GPU to generate building layouts, and an analytic sine-wave curve to define the road centerline, ensuring perfect synchronization between the rendered visual and the camera path [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Inputs:** Camera state (position, orthonormal basis vectors: right, up, forward), time, resolution, and fixed procedural constants (road parameters, block dimensions) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Outputs:** A color buffer representing the rendered frame [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Inputs:** Frame time (`uTime`), camera state (`uCamPos`, `uCamRight`, `uCamUp`, `uCamForward`), and resolution (`uResolution`).
+*   **Outputs:** A color buffer representing the rendered city scene.
 
 **Data Structures:**
-*   **Ray Basis:** A structure containing `position` (vec3), `right` (vec3), `up` (vec3), and `forward` (vec3) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Cell Coordinates:** Integer triplets `(cx, cy, cz)` representing the grid cell of a building block [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Hash Seed:** A 32-bit unsigned integer derived from cell coordinates [S1, unofficial].
+The scene is defined implicitly. No vertex buffers or texture arrays are used for geometry. The only persistent data structures are the uniform variables passed from the CPU and the local variables within the fragment shader.
 
